{{also|FET}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/fɛt/}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{term|fetten|lang=enm}}, {{term|feten|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|ang|en}} {{term|fetian||lang=ang}}, {{term|fatian||to bring, fetch|lang=ang}}, probably a conflation of {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{term/t|gem-pro|*fetaną||to go}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{term/t|ine-pro|*pÁd-||to go}}; and {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{term/t|gem-pro|*fatōną||to hold, seize}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{term/t|ine-pro|*pAd-||to seize}}. Cognate with Dutch {{term|vatten||to catch, grab|lang=nl}}, {{langname|de}} {{term|fassen||to lay hold of, seize, take, hold|lang=de}}. Compare also {{langname|is}} {{term|feta||to find one's way|lang=is}}. More at {{l/en|fetch}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb|fets|fetting|fet}}

# {{context|obsolete|lang=en}} To [[fetch]].

===Etymology 2===
Compare {{term|feat|lang=en}}, {{etyl|fr|-}} {{term|fait|lang=fr}}, and {{etyl|it|-}} {{term|fetta|lang=it||slice}}, {{etyl|de|-}} {{term|Fetzen|lang=de||rag}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{context|obsolete|lang=en}} A [[piece]].
#: {{rfquotek|Dryton}}

{{Webster 1913}}

